# Server-Side Data Fetching

Fetch data on the server where it makes sense (SEO, secrets/API keys, large
payloads, first-paint data). Ask me to validate the approach before wiring it up.

IMPORTANT — this project uses the Next.js **App Router** (`app/`), so there is
no `getServerSideProps`. The App Router equivalent is an **async Server
Component** that fetches directly (optionally with `export const dynamic = "force-dynamic"`
for per-request data). Only use `getServerSideProps` if a file lives under a
Pages Router `pages/` directory.

- Default route files to Server Components. Add `"use client"` only when you need
  hooks, browser APIs, or interactivity, and push it as far down the tree as possible.
- Put fetching logic in `lib/get-*.ts` (matching the existing `lib/get-wellness-feed.ts`,
  `lib/get-map-places.ts` pattern) and call it from the server component.
- Before introducing or changing server-side fetching, ask me to confirm:
  the data source, whether it should be per-request vs cached/revalidated, and
  whether the component should stay server or go client.

```tsx
// ✅ App Router: async server component (no getServerSideProps)
export const dynamic = "force-dynamic";

export default async function FeedPage() {
  const feed = await getPersonalizedWellnessFeed(profile);
  return <WellnessFeed data={feed} />;
}
```
